{"abstract":"Ensuring transparency and integrity in political communication on climate change has arguably never been more important than today. Yet we know little about how politicians focus on, talk about, and portray climate change on social media. Here we study it from the perspective of political advertisement. We use Meta’s Ad Library to collect 602,546 ads that have been issued by US Congress members since mid-2018. Out of those only 19,176 (3.2%) are climate-related. Analyzing this data, we find that Democrats focus substantially more on climate change than Republicans, with 99.7% of all climate-related ads stemming from Democrats. In particular, we find this is driven by a small core of Democrats, where 72% of all impressions can be attributed to 10 politicians. Interestingly, we find a significant difference in the average amount of impressions generated per dollar between the two parties. Republicans generate on average 188% more impressions with their climate ads for the same money spent as Democrats. We build models to explain the differences and find that demographic factors only partially explain the variance. Our results demonstrate differences of climate-related advertisements of US congress members and reveal differences in advertising characteristics between the two political parties. We anticipate our work to be a starting point for further studies about climate-related ads on Meta’s platforms.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Aisenpreis, Laurenz","Gyrst, Gustav","Sekara, Vedran"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712524"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Climate change","Computational social science","political advertisement"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how do us congress members advertise climate change an analysis of ads run on meta s platforms","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1609/icwsm.v17i1.22121","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-712524","ids":["itu-632a3dfb-2a2e-4d74-a846-47e698afa7bd"],"keyword":["climate change","computational social science","political advertisement"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-C257FC09C0325FC7B75F611CE36ECF7E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/ICWSM/article/view/22121/21900","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1609/icwsm.v17i1.22121","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Laurenz","key":"aisenpreispau","last":"Aisenpreis","name":"Aisenpreis, Laurenz","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Gustav","key":"gyrstpau","last":"Gyrst","name":"Gyrst, Gustav","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vedran","key":"sekarapau","last":"Sekara","locid":"db64c222-0674-4eab-9f09-7f9ea1c483e3","name":"Sekara, Vedran","orcid":"0000-0003-4490-3757","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1609/icwsm.v17i1.22121","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the International AAAI Conference on Web and Social Media"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-632a3dfb-2a2e-4d74-a846-47e698afa7bd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Do US Congress Members Advertise Climate Change: An Analysis of Ads Run on Meta’s Platforms","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712524","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Large-scale collection of human behavioural data by companies raises serious privacy concerns. We show that behaviour captured in the form of application usage data collected from smartphones is highly unique even in large datasets encompassing millions of individuals. This makes behaviour-based re-identification of users across datasets possible. We study 12 months of data from 3.5 million people from 33 countries and show that although four apps are enough to uniquely re-identify 91.2% of individuals using a simple strategy based on public information, there are considerable seasonal and cultural variations in re-identification rates. We find that people have more unique app-fingerprints during summer months making it easier to re-identify them. Further, we find significant variations in uniqueness across countries, and reveal that American users are the easiest to re-identify, while Finns have the least unique app-fingerprints. We show that differences across countries can largely be explained by two characteristics of the country specific app-ecosystems: the popularity distribution and the size of app-fingerprints. Our work highlights problems with current policies intended to protect user privacy and emphasizes that policies cannot directly be ported between countries. We anticipate this will nuance the discussion around re-identifiability in digital datasets and improve digital privacy.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Sekara, Vedran","Alessandretti, Laura","Mones, Enys","Jonsson, Håkan"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-035769"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Sekara, Vedran"],"country":["Japan","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Lund University","Sony Group Corporation"],"keyword":["Applied Mathematics","Information theory and computation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"temporal and cultural limits of privacy in smartphone app usage","src":["dtu","itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-82294-1","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-100.00-035769","identifier":[{"id":"33594096","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC7887199","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85100941364","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-8a4597e5-cba9-4ad9-9ac1-597d2f951a80","itu-e41c5ea8-34ad-4b9b-935f-0c325e1efca9","ku-3b979611-9047-4228-8225-52c7a47fe8ee"],"keyword":["Applied mathematics","Information theory and computation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-E03DDE621B931C4A47891B40C1F1EA32"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/245550531/s41598_021_82294_1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86251236/Temporal_and_cultural_limits.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-82294-1","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/276269098/Temporal_and_cultural_limits_of_privacy_in_smartphone_app_usage.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"jp","key":"sony group corporation","loc":["Japan","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"Sony Group Corporation","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Cognitive Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Economics, Department of Economics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"se","key":"lund university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Lund University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Vedran","key":"sekarapau","last":"Sekara","locid":"db64c222-0674-4eab-9f09-7f9ea1c483e3","name":"Sekara, Vedran","orcid":"0000-0003-4490-3757","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Laura","key":"alessandrettipau","last":"Alessandretti","locid":"0e9909a3-464d-4dbe-8e3a-05327a3bc245","name":"Alessandretti, Laura","orcid":"0000-0001-6003-1165","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Enys","key":"monespau","last":"Mones","locid":"b31c2af8-dc34-43dc-88a2-5a26ca3e5f21","name":"Mones, Enys","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Håkan","key":"jonssonpau","last":"Jonsson","name":"Jonsson, Håkan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33594096"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-021-82294-1","issn":["20452322"],"journal":"Scientific Reports","pages":"1-9","paperid":"3861 (2021)","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85100941364"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-8a4597e5-cba9-4ad9-9ac1-597d2f951a80","itu-e41c5ea8-34ad-4b9b-935f-0c325e1efca9","ku-3b979611-9047-4228-8225-52c7a47fe8ee"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Temporal and cultural limits of privacy in smartphone app usage","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-100.00-035769","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are a global consensus on the world’s most pressing challenges. They come with a set of 232 indicators against which countries should regularly monitor their progress, ensuring that everyone is represented in up-to-date data that can be used to make decisions to improve people’s lives. However, existing data sources to measure progress on the SDGs are often outdated or lacking appropriate disaggregation. We evaluate the value that anonymous, publicly accessible advertising data from Facebook can provide in mapping socio-economic development in two low and middle income countries, the Philippines and India. Concretely, we show that audience estimates of how many Facebook users in a given location use particular device types, such as Android vs. iOS devices, or particular connection types, such as 2G vs. 4G, provide strong signals for modeling regional variation in the Wealth Index (WI), derived from the Demographic and Health Survey (DHS). We further show that, surprisingly, the predictive power of these digital connectivity features is roughly equal at both the high and low ends of the WI spectrum. Finally we show how such data can be used to create gender-disaggregated predictions, but that these predictions only appear plausible in contexts with gender equal Facebook usage, such as the Philippines, but not in contexts with large gender Facebook gaps, such as India.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Fatehkia, Masoomali","Tingzon, Isabelle","Orden, Ardie","Sy, Stephanie","Sekara, Vedran","Garcia-Herranz, Manuel","Weber, Ingmar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384466"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Middle East","Asia, South","OECD"],"country":["Philippines","Qatar","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Qatar Computing Research institute","Thinking Machines","UNICEF"],"keyword":["Facebook advertising data","Gender data","Poverty mapping","Remote sensing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"mapping socioeconomic indicators using social media advertising data","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1140/epjds/s13688-020-00235-w","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384466","identifier":[{"id":"85088655771","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d93e4cbf-e314-4c07-9ab5-bb4e03035e19"],"keyword":["Facebook advertising data","Gender data","Poverty mapping","Remote sensing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-A0E48445CB5D3F2AF2A91E66374DB76A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1140/epjds/s13688-020-00235-w","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551104/s13688_020_00235_w.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"qa","key":"qatar computing research institute","loc":["Qatar","Asia, Middle East"],"name":"Qatar Computing Research institute","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ph","key":"thinking machines","loc":["Philippines","Asia, South"],"name":"Thinking Machines","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"unicef","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"UNICEF","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Masoomali","key":"fatehkiapau","last":"Fatehkia","name":"Fatehkia, Masoomali","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Isabelle","key":"tingzonpau","last":"Tingzon","name":"Tingzon, Isabelle","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ardie","key":"ordenpau","last":"Orden","name":"Orden, Ardie","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stephanie","key":"sypau","last":"Sy","name":"Sy, Stephanie","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Vedran","key":"sekarapau","last":"Sekara","locid":"db64c222-0674-4eab-9f09-7f9ea1c483e3","name":"Sekara, Vedran","orcid":"0000-0003-4490-3757","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Manuel","key":"garciaherranzpau","last":"Garcia-Herranz","name":"Garcia-Herranz, Manuel","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingmar","key":"weberpau","last":"Weber","name":"Weber, Ingmar","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1140/epjds/s13688-020-00235-w","issn":["21931127"],"journal":"EPJ Data Science","paperid":"22","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"EPJ Data Science"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85088655771"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d93e4cbf-e314-4c07-9ab5-bb4e03035e19"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Mapping Socioeconomic Indicators Using Social Media Advertising Data","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384466","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Recent seminal works on human mobility have shown that individuals constantly exploit a small set of repeatedly visited locations. A concurrent literature has emphasized the explorative nature of human behavior, showing that the number of visited places grows steadily over time. How to reconcile these seemingly contradicting facts remains an open question. Here, we analyze high-resolution multi-year traces of $\\sim$40,000 individuals from 4 datasets and show that this tension vanishes when the long-term evolution of mobility patterns is considered. We reveal that mobility patterns evolve significantly yet smoothly, and that the number of familiar locations an individual visits at any point is a conserved quantity with a typical size of $\\sim$25 locations. We use this finding to improve state-of-the-art modeling of human mobility. Furthermore, shifting the attention from aggregated quantities to individual behavior, we show that the size of an individual's set of preferred locations correlates with the number of her social interactions. This result suggests a connection between the conserved quantity we identify, which as we show can not be understood purely on the basis of time constraints, and the `Dunbar number' describing a cognitive upper limit to an individual's number of social relations. We anticipate that our work will spark further research linking the study of Human Mobility and the Cognitive and Behavioral Sciences.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Alessandretti, Laura","Sapiezynski, Piotr","Sekara, Vedran","Lehmann, Sune","Baronchelli, Andrea"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-036450"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Lehmann, Sune"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","City, University of London","University of London"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evidence for a conserved quantity in human mobility","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/151582090/1609.03526.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S02-100.00-036450","identifier":[{"id":"000446612000018","type":"wos"},{"id":"31097800","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85048657389","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-a9e9d295-8d50-484d-aae5-187b639ee287","ku-4291de5e-1f2a-4f6b-b7cb-a47499f98829"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-BF94986BB41242A241BF7A2D350210B1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/151582090/1609.03526.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"https://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/license.html","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.03526.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Cognitive Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"city university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"City, University of London","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Niels Bohr Institute, Biocomplexity"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of London","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Laura","key":"alessandrettipau","last":"Alessandretti","locid":"0e9909a3-464d-4dbe-8e3a-05327a3bc245","name":"Alessandretti, Laura","orcid":"0000-0001-6003-1165","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Piotr","key":"sapiezynskipau","last":"Sapiezynski","locid":"ad5149fa-a8ee-4f2d-8e36-8a5da45b21c3","name":"Sapiezynski, Piotr","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Vedran","key":"sekarapau","last":"Sekara","locid":"2a6bd001-485b-4d3e-8210-d3ce9420c177","name":"Sekara, Vedran","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sune","key":"lehmannpau","last":"Lehmann","locid":"9049a0bd-7e6f-4f06-80f6-88ac447289e7","name":"Lehmann, Sune","orcid":"0000-0001-6099-2345","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrea","key":"baronchellipau","last":"Baronchelli","name":"Baronchelli, Andrea","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31097800"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41562-018-0364-x","issn":["23973374"],"journal":"Nature Human Behaviour","pages":"485-491","publisher":"Nature","status":"p","title":"Nature Human Behaviour","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85048657389"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-a9e9d295-8d50-484d-aae5-187b639ee287","ku-4291de5e-1f2a-4f6b-b7cb-a47499f98829"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evidence for a Conserved Quantity in Human Mobility","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-036450","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Experience plays a critical role in crafting high-impact scientific work. This is particularly evident in top multidisciplinary journals, where a scientist is unlikely to appear as senior author if he or she has not previously published within the same journal. Here, we develop a quantitative understanding of author order by quantifying this \"chaperone effect,\" capturing how scientists transition into senior status within a particular publication venue. We illustrate that the chaperone effect has a different magnitude for journals in different branches of science, being more pronounced in medical and biological sciences and weaker in natural sciences. Finally, we show that in the case of high-impact venues, the chaperone effect has significant implications, specifically resulting in a higher average impact relative to papers authored by new principal investigators (PIs). Our findings shed light on the role played by experience in publishing within specific scientific journals, on the paths toward acquiring the necessary experience and expertise, and on the skills required to publish in prestigious venues.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Sekara, Vedran","Deville, Pierre","Ahnert, Sebastian E","Barabási, Albert-László","Sinatra, Roberta","Lehmann, Sune"],"cuid":["S02-089.97-034332"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Lehmann, Sune"],"country":["Belgium","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Northeastern University","University of Cambridge","Université catholique de Louvain"],"keyword":["Mentorship","Science of science","Scientific careers"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"rev